<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec_lc-circuits" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  	<title>LC Circuits</title>

	<assemblage component="implementation">
  <title>Implementation Notes</title>
  <p>
	The activities in this section are designed to be implemented in an interactive studio classroom, after students have completed <xref ref="sec_RC-circuits"/>, <xref ref="sec_LR-circuits"/>, and their prerequisite sections.  Together with the activity in <xref ref="sec_using-dynamic-circuits"/>, they take about two hours.
	</p>
  </assemblage>
		
		<p>Recall that the voltage across an inductor is given by <me>V_{inductor} = -L \frac{dI}{dt}</me></p>
		

		<activity>
	<title>Exploring LR and RC Circuits</title>
	<introduction>
		<p></p>
	<p>
		The circuit below contains a battery connected to two branches: one with a resistor and a capacitor, the other with a resistor and an inductor.  The switch closes at <m>t = 0</m>.
	</p>
		<figure xml:id="x_fig_RL-RC">
			<title>LR and RC Circuit Diagram</title>
    		<caption>A circuit with a battery, a resistor, and an inductor.</caption>
    		<image source="LR-RC.png" width = "60%" ><description>A circuit diagram with a battery, an open switch, and two parallel branches: on the left a resistor and capacitor in series, on the right a resistor and inductor in series.</description></image>
		</figure>
	</introduction>
	<task>
	<statement>
		<p>
			Describe how the current changes in each branch using a physical argument.  Make sure that the capacitor and inductor continuity conditions are met!  Sketch graphs that agree with your answers.
		</p>
	</statement>
	</task>
	<task>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Suppose each branch has the same time constant.  Write an equation relating <m>L</m> and <m>C</m>, assuming the resistors are identical.
			</p>
		</statement>
	</task>
	<task>
		<statement>
			<p>
				What would happen if the time constants were different?
			</p>
		</statement>
	</task>
	<task>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Sketch a graph of the current as a function of time.
			</p>
		</statement>
	</task>
	<task>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Try building this circuit in the <q>RLC</q> tab of the <url href="https://phet.colorado.edu/sims/html/circuit-construction-kit-ac/latest/circuit-construction-kit-ac_all.html">Circuits PhET simulation</url> to check your predictions.
			</p>
		</statement>
	</task>
</activity>

<activity>
	<title>LC Circuits</title>
	<introduction>
		<p></p>
	<p>
		The circuit contains a capacitor <m>C</m> with initial charge <m>Q_o</m> and an inductor <m>L</m>.  The switch closes at <m>t = 0</m>.
	</p>
		<figure xml:id="x_fig_LC">
			<title>LC Circuit Diagram</title>
    		<caption>A circuit with a capacitor and an inductor.</caption>
    		<image source="LC-circui2.png" width = "60%" ><description>A circuit diagram with an initially charged capacitor (positive on top), a switch, and an inductor.</description></image>
		</figure>
	</introduction>
	<task>
	<statement>
		<p>
			At each of the following instants, determine the sign of (1) the voltage across each element and (2) the current through each element.
			<ol marker="a.">
				<li>
					<p>
						Just before the switch is closed.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						Just after the switch is closed.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						At the first moment the charge on the capacitor is zero.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						At the moment when the upper plate of the capacitor has the most negative charge.
					</p>
				</li>
			</ol>
		</p>
	</statement>
	</task>
	<task>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Sketch a graph of the current through the circuit consistent with your answers above.
			</p>
		</statement>
	</task>
	<task>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Try building this circuit in the <q>RLC</q> tab of the <url href="https://phet.colorado.edu/sims/html/circuit-construction-kit-ac/latest/circuit-construction-kit-ac_all.html">Circuits PhET simulation</url> to check your predictions.
			</p>
		</statement>
	</task>
</activity>


  	<subsubsection><title>Derivation: LC Circuit</title></subsubsection>

    <corollary xml:id="vid-lc-circuits">
	<title>LC Circuits</title>
	
	<statement>
		<p>
			
		</p>

		<video youtube="EZmh2TOI-WI"><description>A description of LC circuits.</description></video>

	</statement>
  </corollary>

	<p>
		You can use the <xref ref="loop-rule"/> to relate the voltages across the different circuit elements: 
		<me>V_C + V_L = 0</me>
		<me>\frac{Q}{C} + L\frac{dI}{dt} = 0</me>
		<me>Q(t) = -LC\frac{d^2Q}{dt^2}</me>
		The charge on the capacitor therefore fits the model for <xref ref="assumption-shm"/>!
		<me>I(t) = \frac{V}{R} \left(1 - e^{-tR/L}\right)</me>
	</p>

<activity>
	<title>LC Harmonics</title>
	<introduction>
		<p></p>
	<p>
		Using the simple harmonic motion model:
	</p>
	</introduction>
	<task>
	<statement>
		<p>
			Write an equation for the angular frequency.
		</p>
	</statement>
	</task>
	<task>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Calculate the current as a function of time.
			</p>
		</statement>
	</task>
	<task>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Graph the current as a function of time.
			</p>
		</statement>
	</task>
	<task>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Resolve any inconsistencies between your graph and your previous answers.
			</p>
		</statement>
	</task>
	<task>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Make sense of your graph by considering how it would change if you made changes to each of these three quantities: <m>C</m>, <m>L</m>, <m>Q_o</m>.
			</p>
		</statement>
	</task>
</activity>
			
<activity>
	<title>Harmonic Motion Analogy</title>
	<introduction>
		<p></p>
	<p>
		The following two systems are both examples of simple harmonic motion: <xref ref="mass_spring"/> and <xref ref="x_fig_LC"/>
	</p>
	</introduction>
	<task>
	<statement>
		<p>
			Write the Law of Motion for the block on a spring and represent it as the differential equation for simple harmonic motion
		</p>
	</statement>
	</task>
	<task>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Use the block on a spring as an analogy for the LC circuit:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<p>
						What feature of the block on a spring is the capacitance analogous to?
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						What feature of the block on a spring is the inductance analogous to?
					</p>
				</li>
			</ul>
		</statement>
	</task>
	<task>
		<statement>
			<p>
				For each system, explicitly identify any important assumptions you are making.
			</p>
		</statement>
	</task>
</activity>
	



  

	
